.TH CARDEA-TEAM 1 "November 2026" "cardea-kernel" "Cardea"
.SH NAME
cardea-team \- run the agent dev team from the cardea command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cardea team
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cardea team
runs the dev team described by the host file
.IR /etc/cardea/team.host.json .
It acts as the team's supervisor. The supervisor's identity belongs to the
.B cardea-kernel
account, so the command passes each request to
.I /usr/libexec/cardea-team-run
through a sudoers rule shipped in the package
.RI ( /etc/sudoers.d/cardea-team ).
You do not type
.BR "sudo -u" .
The helper runs the same code as the kernel, from the package.
It decides nothing about authority: the kernel checks the supervisor's live authority on every read and write, and a refusal is reported by its error code.
The commands print no credential.
.PP
Your account must be in the
.B cardea-operators
group (or be root):
.B adduser
.I USER
.BR cardea-operators ,
then log in again.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show members and their runs, work by state, mail, approvals, spend against the ceilings, when each credential expires, and the last failed runs.
While the team is paused it prints the pause reason instead.
.TP
.BR pause " [" \fIreason\fR ]
The kill switch. Disable the supervisor: the kernel cancels every unfinished run and nothing is admitted until
.BR resume .
The reason is kept for
.BR status .
.TP
.B resume
Enable the supervisor again. Triggers admit from their next slot or event.
.TP
.B stop
Pause, then delete the group. Collections and records stay. Asks to confirm (\fB\-\-yes\fR skips it).
.TP
.B apply
Put the collections and the group to the kernel as the supervisor.
.TP
.BR render " [\-\-out " \fIDIR\fR ]
Print the documents the host file renders, for review, or write them to
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR provision " [\-\-paused]"
Write the supervisor's grants into the provisioning document and provision them.
.TP
.B settings
Show the host settings in plain words: developers, model, run and spend limits, schedules, the email rate limit and the product goals.
.TP
.BI set " KEY VALUE"
Change a setting. It validates like the dashboard's Settings page, shows the diff, asks to confirm and then applies it (render, provision, apply, audit).
Without a terminal it changes nothing unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. Keys:
.RS
.nf
runs.team N, runs.ROLE N          runs a day
usd.team X, usd.run X, usd.ROLE X dollars a day, or one run
schedule.ROLE "CRON; CRON"        five-field cron; "none" clears
email.rate COUNT/DAYS             email rate limit, e.g. 10/1
developers N                      number of developers
model NAME                        the model
goals TEXT | @FILE                product goals
non-goals TEXT | @FILE            what the team must not build
.fi
ROLE is pm, tl, dev or pe.
.RE
.TP
.BI mail " TARGET TEXT"
Send supervisor mail to
.BR tl ", " pm ", " pe ", " ux
(the role's pool) or
.BI dev. N
(one developer). Text
.B \-
is read from standard input.
.TP
.BR "mail list" " [\-\-limit N] [\-\-member ID] [\-\-item COLLECTION/ID]"
List the team's mail with full text, newest first.
.PP
.B \-\-host
.I FILE
selects another host file, but only when run as
.BR cardea-kernel ;
through the helper the host file is always the default, and it must be owned by root or
.B cardea-kernel
and writable by no one else.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a refusal or failure; 2 when a command that needs a confirmation was not confirmed because there was no terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cardea/team.host.json
The host file.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/cardea-team
Lets the cardea-operators group run
.I /usr/libexec/cardea-team-run
as cardea-kernel, with no arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cardea (1)
